# The dependency tree has one unresolvable peer conflict: `@auth/core@0.24` declares
# `nodemailer@^6`, while `next-auth@4.24` declares `nodemailer@^7`. Both are peerOptional and
# nodemailer is only pulled in if email auth is configured, so the conflict is inert at runtime —
# but npm 10 refuses to resolve it and fails outright.
#
# Without this file, behaviour depends on which npm you happen to run: recent npm (11.x) resolves it
# silently, npm 10 (Docker images, CI runners) errors with ERESOLVE. That difference is exactly why
# `package-lock.json` drifted out of sync and `npm ci` broke in Docker while working locally.
#
# Setting it here makes local, CI and the production image agree.
#
# ⚠️ CONSEQUENCE: with this flag npm does NOT auto-install peer dependencies. Anything the app
# actually needs must be declared explicitly in package.json, even when it is "only" a peer of
# something else. `@trpc/react-query` is the cautionary tale — it is a required peer of
# `@trpc/next`, nothing imports it directly, and without it the tRPC client type silently collapses
# into a union of collision-guard error strings, so `trpc.match` resolves to String.prototype.match
# and every router call fails to type-check. Declared explicitly for exactly that reason.
#
# Removing this flag is not a one-liner: the tree currently has at least two unresolvable peer
# conflicts (nodemailer above, and prisma-json-types-generator@3.6 wanting @prisma/client@^6 while
# the project is on ^5). Fixing those is a dependency-upgrade project, not a build tweak.
legacy-peer-deps=true
